# Data Architecture

## Overview

The warehouse follows the **Medallion Architecture** (Bronze → Silver → Gold), implemented as three schemas inside a single SQL Server database (`UKRetailBankingDW`). Each layer has one job and one job only — this keeps the pipeline debuggable: if a business question gives a wrong answer, you know exactly which layer to check first.

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    subgraph SRC["Source Systems"]
        direction LR
        S1[Core Banking System]
        S2[CRM Platform]
        S3[Loan Management System]
        S4[Complaint Portal]
        S5[Branch Operations System]
    end

    subgraph BZ["bronze schema"]
        B1[("6 raw tables\nall NVARCHAR\nno transformation")]
    end

    subgraph SV["silver schema"]
        S6[("6 cleaned tables\ndeduplicated, typed,\nvalidity-flagged")]
    end

    subgraph GD["gold schema"]
        G1[("5 dimensions\n3 facts\n4 views")]
    end

    SRC --> BZ --> SV --> GD --> BI[Power BI Dashboard]
```

## Layer responsibilities

| Layer | Responsibility | What it must NOT do |
|---|---|---|
| **Bronze** | Land the source CSVs exactly as received. One table per source file, `NVARCHAR` everywhere so a load never fails on bad data. | No cleaning, no casting, no business logic. |
| **Silver** | Deduplicate, standardize casing, parse mixed date formats, cast numeric fields, validate (flag, don't delete). | No aggregation, no dimensional modeling. Rows are kept even when flagged invalid — silver is the audit trail. |
| **Gold** | Model as a star schema (dimensions + facts), then expose business-ready views on top. Only clean/valid rows make it into facts. | No raw text wrangling — anything messy should already be resolved by silver. |

## Why SQL Server + a single database (not separate DBs per layer)

- Schemas (`bronze`/`silver`/`gold`) give the same logical separation as separate databases, with simpler cross-layer joins during ETL (silver reads directly from bronze, gold from silver, no linked servers needed).
- `TRUNCATE + INSERT` inside stored procedures makes every layer safely re-runnable — rerunning the whole pipeline from scratch always produces the same result.

## Load orchestration

Each layer has its own stored procedure (`bronze.load_bronze`, `silver.load_silver`, `gold.load_gold`), run in that order. Each procedure:
1. Truncates its own tables (never touches the layer above or below)
2. Loads/transforms with `PRINT` statements marking progress and duration
3. Wraps everything in `TRY...CATCH` so a failure produces a clear error rather than a partial silent load

See `docs/data_flow.md` for the table-by-table lineage and `run_pipeline.ps1` for full automation.
